The baseline is already a single ciphertext-ciphertext multiply, which is the minimum for computing x² on encrypted data. `square(c)` expresses the same operation directly and may lower to a cheaper squaring path, since it can skip some of the work a general multiply does. I'll use that.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = square(x);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The `square(x)` attempt came out at 67.50 ms against 64.64 ms for the baseline. That difference is most likely measurement noise, since both versions compile to one multiply, one relinearization and one rescale. The minimum operation count for x² is already reached, so there is little left to remove.

I considered rewriting x² as ((x+x)² - ... ) or splitting it into other forms, but each of those adds multiplications or plaintext operations and can't beat one squaring. Without plaintext constants or extra inputs, no cheaper algebraic route exists. I'm going back to the baseline form, which is the best result so far.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Ciphertext y = x * x;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline form is already minimal: one ciphertext-ciphertext multiply, one relinearization and one rescale. The two measurements (64.64 ms and 65.06 ms) differ only by noise, and `square(x)` gave 67.50 ms. I haven't found a variant that removes any operation, since the DSL has no constants or extra inputs to build a cheaper route to x². Any alternative, such as splitting x into parts or using rotations, adds work.

